🧠 Concept
Controls:

Who can access AKS (users)
What they can do (RBAC)
How pods access Azure securely (no secrets)


🏗️ Real Architecture
User → Azure AD → AKS → RBAC
Pod → ServiceAccount → OIDC → Managed Identity


🛠️ Hands-on Implementation
✅ Step 1: Enable Azure AD + RBAC in Terraform

resource "azurerm_kubernetes_cluster" "aks" {
  name                = "aks-secure"
  location            = var.location
  resource_group_name = var.rg

  azure_active_directory_role_based_access_control {
    managed                = true
    azure_rbac_enabled     = true
  }

  identity {
    type = "SystemAssigned"
  }

✅ Step 2: Create Namespace-Limited Access
Create namespace: kubectl create namespace dev

Role:
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  namespace: dev
  name: developer-role
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "create"]

Bind:
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: dev-binding
  namespace: dev
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: user@company.com
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: developer-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
``
✅ Step 3: Workload Identity (Pod → Azure)

Enable in Aks:
oidc_issuer_enabled       = true
workload_identity_enabled = true

Create identity:
az identity create --name aks-identity --resource-group rg


apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: workload-sa
  annotations:
    azure.workload.identity/client-id: <client-id>

🚨 What You Achieved
✅ No passwords
✅ Secure pod access
✅ Least privilege

